# Groundwater: Specific Yield from Space and from Bores

In Assignment II you took the total water storage that GRACE measures, subtracted the water in
the soil, and called the rest groundwater. A bore measures something different: the height of
the water in one hole in the ground. If the water table falls by one metre and the aquifer
gives up 50 mm of water, the aquifer has a specific yield of 0.05. This assignment puts the two
measurements side by side at ten monitoring sites in the southern Murray-Darling Basin and asks
whether their ratio really is a specific yield. The calculation is short. Most of the marks are
for finding out what the number means, and where it stops meaning anything.

**Total: 15 marks.**

For EMSC3025, Questions 1–5 total 15 marks. Question 6 is an optional one-mark bonus.

For EMSC6025, Questions 1–3 total 8 marks, Question 4 is worth 3 marks, Question 5 is worth 2 marks, and required Question 6 is worth 2 marks.

### Computer labs for this assignment

- [Lab 3: xarray gridded data](https://colab.research.google.com/github/water-course/colab-tutorials/blob/main/lab03_xarray_gridded_data.ipynb) covers coordinate selection and the latitude-direction trap, which you need again for the GRACE and soil moisture grids.
- [Lab 5: Rain gauge interpolation](https://colab.research.google.com/github/water-course/colab-tutorials/blob/main/lab05_rain_gauge_interpolation.ipynb) covers inverse-distance weighting, which Question 5 uses.

Your Assignment II notebook already reads both gridded files. Reuse that code.

.whitespace{10pt}

.center
   .container width:{92%} background:{.lightblue} radius:{9pt} padding:{10pt}
      ##### The idea

      When the water table in an unconfined aquifer falls by $ \Delta h $, the pores that
      drain release a depth of water $ \Delta S = S_y \, \Delta h $, where $ S_y $ is the
      specific yield. So

      $ S_y = \frac{\Delta S}{\Delta h} $ {#equation-1}

      GRACE minus soil moisture gives $ \Delta S $ as a depth of water. A bore gives
      $ \Delta h $ as a change in water level. Both are in metres once you convert, and
      $ S_y $ has no units. The aquifers lecture gives $ S_y $ between 0.01 and 0.30 for
      unconfined aquifers and a storativity $ S $ between $ 10^{-5} $ and $ 10^{-3} $ for
      confined aquifers. Keep those two ranges in mind from the start.

### The data

#### Bore water levels

The bore records come from the WaterNSW [realtime data website](https://realtimedata.waternsw.com.au/water.stm).
A fixed copy for this assignment, one CSV file per pipe with a site table and a README, is on
the course data server:

.row alignment:{center}
   [https://data.gadopt.org/water-course/waternsw_bores_2003-2025.zip](https://data.gadopt.org/water-course/waternsw_bores_2003-2025.zip)

Use this copy, so that everybody works from the same data. The column `depth_below_mp_m` is the
depth in metres from the measuring point at the top of the bore down to the water. Read the
README in the zip before you start.

A monitoring site can have several pipes. Each pipe is a separate tube in the same borehole,
open to the ground over a different depth interval, so two pipes at one site can measure two
different aquifers. Use the pipe given in .ref {table-1} for Questions 1–5.

| Location | Site | Pipe | Latitude | Longitude |
|:---------|:-----|:----:|:---------|:----------|
| Toganmain | GW036275 | 1 | -34.6736 | 145.6233 |
| Amberdell | GW036582 | 2 | -35.8116 | 144.8309 |
| Barratta | GW036871 | 3 | -35.2736 | 144.5336 |
| Howlong | GW273166 | 1 | -35.9735 | 146.6186 |
| Wagga Wagga | GW273167 | 1 | -35.1006 | 147.3692 |
| Narrandera | GW040956 | 3 | -34.6966 | 146.3422 |
| Jerilderie | GW036211 | 1 | -35.0658 | 145.3797 |
| McLennons Bore Rd | GW403747 | 3 | -35.0293 | 145.5936 |
| Forbes | GW036611 | 1 | -33.7553 | 147.5824 |
| Roeta Rd | GW036284 | 2 | -33.3522 | 145.0312 |
{#table-1}

The zip also holds a second pipe at Toganmain (pipe 2), Barratta (pipe 2) and Wagga Wagga
(pipe 2). Question 6 uses them.

#### GRACE and soil moisture

Use the same two files as Assignment II: the CSR RL06.3 mascons
(`CSR_GRACE_GRACE-FO_RL0603_Mascons_all-corrections.nc`) and the ESA CCI soil moisture subset
(`esacci_sm_combined_v09-1_MDB_daily_2003-2023.nc`). Every warning in Assignment II about these
two files still applies.

.whitespace{10pt}

.center
   .container width:{92%} background:{rgb(255, 215, 215)} radius:{9pt} padding:{10pt} borderwidth:{1pt}
      ##### Six ways this will go wrong without an error message

      **1. Depth is not level.** A larger `depth_below_mp_m` means the water is *lower*. If you
      use depth where the equation needs level, every $ S_y $ comes out negative. Define the
      water level as $ h = -\text{depth} $ before you do anything else.

      **2. Millimetres against metres.** GRACE and soil moisture give $ \Delta S $ in mm of
      water. The bores give $ \Delta h $ in m. If you do not convert, $ S_y $ is a thousand
      times too large.

      **3. The bore records are short.** At most sites the telemetry starts in 2009–2011.
      Before that, some files contain daily values interpolated between occasional manual
      readings (quality code 26). The soil moisture file ends in December 2023. Use **January
      2010 to December 2023** for all fitting.

      **4. GRACE has gaps.** Of the 168 months from 2010 to 2023, 32 have no GRACE solution,
      including every month from July 2017 to May 2018. Average all three datasets to calendar
      months, match on year and month, and do not fill the gaps.

      **5. The GRACE traps from Assignment II.** The time axis does not decode (`Units` with a
      capital U), `lwe_thickness` is in cm, latitude runs south to north, and the anomalies are
      relative to the 2004–2009 mean. Remove the 2004–2009 mean from the soil moisture before
      you subtract it. Select the grid cell with `method="nearest"` on latitude and longitude
      only, never in the same call as a time selection.

      **6. Do not divide month by month.** The ratio of one month's $ \Delta S $ to that month's
      $ \Delta h $ becomes very large whenever $ \Delta h $ is close to zero, and the mean of
      such ratios means nothing. Question 3 tells you how to estimate $ S_y $ from the whole
      record.

### Questions

1. **(2 marks)** Read the ten bore records in .ref {table-1}, average them to calendar months
   (state the minimum number of days you require in a month), and convert them to water level
   $ h $ with up positive. Plot the monthly water level at all ten sites, with dates on the
   time axis and units on the level axis.

   Make a table with, for each site, the first and last month of the record, the number of
   months with a value in 2010–2023, the mean depth to water in 2010–2023, and the median
   within-year range of the monthly level (the maximum minus the minimum in each calendar
   year). Describe what you see. Which sites swing by several metres every year, in which
   season is the water lowest, and what do you think causes it? Which sites show short upward
   spikes, and in which years? Look at where those sites are.

2. **(3 marks)** Using the method of Assignment II, build a monthly series of groundwater
   storage change $ \Delta S $ in mm at each of the ten sites: GRACE total water storage change
   in the grid cell nearest the site, minus the soil moisture change in the soil moisture grid
   cell nearest the site. Take $ L = 20 $ cm for the soil layer. Plot the ten series together.

   Then test how much GRACE can tell the ten sites apart.

   - Count how many of the ten GRACE series are different from each other, and explain why
     some are identical.
   - Compute the correlation of $ \Delta S $ between every pair of sites over the months that
     all ten share, and report the range.
   - Compare the standard deviation *between* the ten sites in a given month, averaged over
     all months, with the standard deviation *in time* at one site, averaged over all sites.

   The grid spacing is 0.25 degrees, the mascons are estimated on a 1 degree grid, and GRACE
   resolves features of about 300 km. How large is the area that the ten sites cover? What do
   your three numbers mean for the map you will make in Question 5?

3. **(3 marks)** Estimate $ S_y $ at each site with .ref {equation-1}. Use the months from
   January 2010 to December 2023 in which both $ \Delta S $ and $ h $ have a value. Subtract
   from each series its own mean over those months, convert $ \Delta S $ to metres, and fit
   $ \Delta S = S_y \, \Delta h $ plus a constant by least squares. The slope is your estimate.

   Report, for each site, $ S_y $ with its standard error, the correlation coefficient $ r $
   and the number of months used. Show the scatter plot with the fitted line for at least one
   site.

4. **(EMSC3025: 4 marks; EMSC6025: 3 marks)** Are these numbers specific yield? Answer with
   evidence from your own results.

   - Place each value against the ranges in the aquifers lecture: 0.01 to 0.30 for unconfined
     aquifers, $ 10^{-5} $ to $ 10^{-3} $ for confined aquifers.
   - Compare the estimate at each site with its within-year range from Question 1. Explain
     the relation you find. Think about what pumping from a confined or leaky aquifer does to
     the water level and what it does to the water stored in the ground.
   - $ \Delta S $ is everything GRACE sees minus 20 cm of soil. Lake Hume, a few tens of
     kilometres upstream of Howlong, holds about 3,000 GL when full. Calculate what the change
     from nearly empty to full is in mm spread over a square 300 km on a side, and compare it
     with the variation of $ \Delta S $ at Howlong. Name the other water that the subtraction
     assigns to groundwater.
   - One GRACE value represents an area of the order of 90,000 km². One bore represents the
     aquifer within some hundreds of metres of it. State what your estimates can be used for
     and what they cannot.

5. **(EMSC3025: 3 marks; EMSC6025: 2 marks)** Make a map of $ S_y $ from your ten values with
   two interpolation methods, for example inverse-distance weighting (Lab 5) and a
   thin-plate spline or linear interpolation. Show the map only inside the convex hull of the
   ten sites, and mark the sites on it. State the parameters of each method.

   Test the maps with leave-one-out cross-validation: remove one site, predict its value from
   the other nine, repeat for every site, and report the root-mean-square error of each method.
   Compare these errors with the standard deviation of the ten values and with the error you
   get if you predict each site with the mean of the other nine. What can the map show, and
   what can it not show?

.whitespace{10pt}

.center
   .container width:{92%} background:{.lightgreen} radius:{9pt} padding:{10pt} borderwidth:{1pt}
      **EMSC6025: required and worth 2 marks.** **EMSC3025: optional and worth one bonus mark.**

6. **Two pipes, and how far the estimate moves.**

   - Repeat Question 3 for the second pipe at Toganmain, Barratta and Wagga Wagga. Compare the
     two pipes at each site: the mean depth to water, the within-year range and $ S_y $.
     Argue which pipe is open to the deeper, more confined part of the aquifer. The screen
     depths are not in the data, so say what your argument rests on.
   - Least squares assumes that all the error is in $ \Delta S $. Repeat Question 3 at all ten
     sites with annual means instead of monthly means, with $ L = 5 $ cm and $ L = 100 $ cm,
     and with the regression reversed (fit $ \Delta h $ against $ \Delta S $ and invert the
     slope). Explain why noise in the bore level biases the ordinary slope towards zero. Give
     a range for $ S_y $ at each site, and say which of the choices changes the estimate most.

### Submission

.center
   .container width:{88%} background:{.lightblue} radius:{9pt} padding:{10pt} borderwidth:{1pt}
      - Submit your answers through the Canvas portal.
      - Submit **one Jupyter notebook** with your code, your figures and your written answers.
        Put the written answers in markdown cells, not in `print` statements.
      - The notebook must **run end to end** from a fresh kernel without errors. Read all data
        from the URLs given above. A path on your own computer or your Google Drive makes the
        notebook impossible to run for anyone else.
      - Label every axis with its quantity and unit.
      - If you use a different data source, or make a different choice from the one the
        question states, say so and give the reason. That is allowed. Doing it without saying
        so is not.
